  "question": {
    "text": "Among the following, which is the strongest oxidizing agent?"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Mn</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Ti</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Fe</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Cr</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The substance which will have the highest&nbsp;value of reduction potential will be the strongest oxidising agent. The values of reduction potential are as follow</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable columnalign=\"right left right left right left right left right left right left\" columnspacing=\"0em 2em 0em 2em 0em 2em 0em 2em 0em 2em 0em\" rowspacing=\"3pt\"><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mrow><msup><mi>Mn</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>Mn</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.57</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mrow><msup><mi>Ti</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>Ti</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mo>&#8722;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.37</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mrow><msup><mi>Fe</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>Fe</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.77</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mrow><msup><mi>Cr</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>Cr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mo>&#8722;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.41</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></p><p>As we can see&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Mn</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></math>&nbsp;has the highest&nbsp;value of standard reduction potential, so&nbsp;it will be the strongest oxidising agent.</p>"
